PAGES=[page('104_page_2.jpg',200,[heading('7.7 Concurrency of Straight Lines'),paragraph('Before to touch the concurrency of straight lines, we need to develop the concept of intersection of lines. Logically, the solution of the system of lines exists only, if the lines intersect.'),paragraph('For illustration, the two lines x+y = 1 and x−y = 0 is forming the system of two linear equations'),eq(r'x+y=1'),eq(r'x-y=0'),paragraph('that in matrix form is represented by Ax = b:'),product([[[1,1],[1,-1]],[['x'],['y']],[[1],[0]]],['','=']),paragraph('The augmented matrix of the system Ax = b is'),product([[[1,1,1],[1,-1,0]]],caption='Source augmented matrix',prefix='A/b ='),paragraph('reduced in an echelon form through row operations'),product([[[1,1,1],[1,-1,0]],[[1,1,1],[0,-2,-1]]],['∼'],suffix='by R₂+R₁(−1)',prefix='A/b ='),paragraph('that gives the system of equations:'),eq(r'x+y=1,\quad-2y=-1,\quad y=\frac12,x=\frac12'),paragraph('The second equation is giving y = 1/2 which is used in first equation to obtain x = 1/2. The solution set (x,y) = (1/2,1/2) of the system of two linear equations is unique (one solution set). This solution is the unique point of intersection at which the given two lines intersect.'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('It is important to note that the system of two lines'),paragraph('• x+y = 1 and x−y = 0 is giving a unique solution set, since the lines are intersecting at just a single point.'),paragraph('• x+y = 1 and x+y = 0 is not giving a solution set, since the lines are not intersecting, because the lines are parallel.'),paragraph('• x+y = 1 and 2x+2y = 2 is giving an infinite set of solutions, since the lines are intersecting at more than one points, because the lines are make a sense of coincident lines.'),heading('7.7.1 Condition of concurrency of three straight lines'),paragraph('The condition of concurrency of three straight lines is the point of intersection at which the three straight lines intersect. For illustration, if the given three lines are'),eq(r'a_1x+b_1y+c_1=0,\quad a_2x+b_2y+c_2=0,\quad a_3x+b_3y+c_3=0','i'),paragraph('then, the three lines develop a homogeneous system of three linear equations'),eq(r'Ax=0'),product([[[r'\(a_1\)',r'\(b_1\)',r'\(c_1\)'],[r'\(a_2\)',r'\(b_2\)',r'\(c_2\)'],[r'\(a_3\)',r'\(b_3\)',r'\(c_3\)']],[['x'],['y'],['1']],[['0'],['0'],['0']]],['','='],suffix='(ii)'),paragraph('In homogeneous system of three linear equations lines (i), the homogeneous coordinates are used:'),product([[['x'],['y']],[['x'],['y'],['1']]],['='],suffix='(iii)',caption='Source homogeneous-coordinate notation'),paragraph('Concurrency means that the three lines must intersect at a point G(x,y), say, that can be found by solving the system of linear equations (i). The system (ii) has a nontrivial solution if the determinant of the coefficient matrix A of the system (ii) is zero:'),note('Homogeneous coordinates embed an ordinary point (x,y) as the triple (x,y,1); the source equality between a two-entry and three-entry column is notation for this embedding, not an equality of vectors in the same dimension. Determinant zero is necessary but by itself does not guarantee finite concurrency: the nonzero homogeneous solution may have its last coordinate zero. For example x = 0, x = 1 and x = 2 are three parallel lines with determinant zero and no common finite point. If two given lines are nonparallel, find their unique finite intersection and test it in the third line; this proves finite concurrency.')]),page('105_page_1.jpg',201,[{'type':'determinant','rows':[[r'\(a_1\)',r'\(b_1\)',r'\(c_1\)'],[r'\(a_2\)',r'\(b_2\)',r'\(c_2\)'],[r'\(a_3\)',r'\(b_3\)',r'\(c_3\)']],'suffix':'= 0 (iv)','caption':'Source determinant condition'},paragraph('This is the condition of concurrency of three lines.'),heading('Remember'),paragraph('For required point of concurrency follow the steps given below:'),paragraph('• Choose any two lines from the given three system of linear equations (i).'),paragraph('• Develop the system of these two linear equations.'),paragraph('• Develop the augmented matrix A/b of the system of two linear equations and reduce it in an echelon form to obtain the point of intersection.'),paragraph('• Substitute the developed point of intersection in the remaining third line. If the point of intersection satisfies the remaining line, then that point of intersection should be taken as the point of concurrency of the given three lines.'),note('Choose two nonparallel lines for a unique finite intersection. Merely choosing any pair may give parallel or coincident lines, which requires a separate check.'),heading('Example 21'),paragraph('Show that the three lines x+4y+3 = 0, 5x−4y−5 = 0 and 2x+2y+1 = 0 are concurrent. If these lines are concurrent, then find out the point of concurrency.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The condition of concurrency (iv) in light of the given three lines is going to be zero:'),{'type':'determinant','rows':[[1,4,3],[5,-4,-5],[2,2,1]],'suffix':'= 1(−4+10)−4(5+10)+3(10+8) = 6−60+54 = 0','caption':'Source Example 21 determinant expansion'},paragraph('The given three lines are concurrent. For the point of concurrency G(x,y), choose the first two lines'),eq(r'x+4y=-3,\quad5x-4y=5'),paragraph('that develops the system of two linear equations, whose augmented matrix A/b is reduced in an echelon form'),product([[[1,4,-3],[5,-4,5]],[[1,4,-3],[0,-24,20]]],['∼'],suffix='by R₂₁(−5) = R₂+R₁(−5)',prefix='A/b ='),paragraph('to obtain the reduced system of linear equations:'),eq(r'x+4y=-3,\quad-24y=20'),paragraph('The second equation is giving y = −5/6 which is used in first equation to obtain x = 1/3. The third line with substitution of the point of intersection (x,y) = (1/3,−5/6) is going to be zero:'),eq(r'2x+2y+1=0\ \Rightarrow\ 2(1/3)+2(-5/6)+1=2/3-5/3+1=0'),paragraph('Thus, the given three lines are concurrent at a point G(1/3,−5/6).'),heading('7.7.2 Equation of median, altitude and right bisector of a triangle'),heading('A. Equation of median of a triangle'),paragraph('A median is a line segment from an interior angle of a triangle to the mid point of the opposite side. Look at the following example, the procedure to find the equation of median of a triangle is illustrated in this example.'),note('A median joins a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side; the source phrase “from an interior angle” means from its vertex. Example 21’s first two lines are nonparallel, and the computed point satisfies the third, establishing actual finite concurrency independently of the determinant test.')])]
