EXAMPLES=[{'num':'21','title':'Finite Concurrency','question':'Show that x+4y+3 = 0, 5x−4y−5 = 0 and 2x+2y+1 = 0 are concurrent, and find their common point.','steps':['Use the first two lines: x+4y = −3 and 5x−4y = 5. Their coefficient determinant is −4−20 = −24 ≠ 0, so they have a unique finite intersection.','Add the equations to obtain 6x = 2, giving x = 1/3.','Substitute in x+4y = −3: 4y = −3−1/3 = −10/3, hence y = −5/6.','Check the first line: 1/3+4(−5/6)+3 = 0.','Check the second line: 5/3−4(−5/6)−5 = 0.','Check the third line: 2/3+2(−5/6)+1 = 2/3−5/3+1 = 0.','Thus all three pass through G(1/3,−5/6), proving finite concurrency.','The three-by-three determinant also equals 6−60+54 = 0, consistent with the independently verified common point. Determinant zero alone would require the finite-point qualification explained in the lesson.'],'answer':'G(1/3,−5/6).','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':201,'jpg':'105_page_1.jpg','verification':'Full source question, matrix reduction and common-point working visually checked'}]}]
